        "title": "Building a Farnsworth Fusor",
        "excerpt":"Background and inspiration   During my Physics 2 honors class, I took it upon myself to complete a dream my brother and I had talked about: let’s build a fusion reactor.   At Miami Dade College, honors courses are evaluated with a capstone project — one that demonstrates mastery of the course material and challenges students to step up to the plate on their own interests in it. This was the excuse I needed.   Fusion was one of the bundle of “human” problems I took an interest in early in my educational journey. As I wrote in the background to IoT TFNA, it was also an apparent prerequisite for the dream of building a mecha. Building one that simply worked, as exposure to the disciplines involved, was more than enough for a capstone.   Process   I took my inspiration from the design published in Make: magazine, Vol. 36 — producing the CAD files, milling the materials, and building out the circuitry as required.   A Farnsworth fusor is an inertial electrostatic confinement device: a grid held at high negative voltage inside a vacuum chamber accelerates deuterium ions inward until some fraction of them collide hard enough to fuse.      A fusor operates at voltages and produces radiation that make it genuinely hazardous. Mine was built as coursework under supervision, and this post is a retrospective rather than a build guide.    Result   I logged much of the process on 3 short video, and edited them together now.      Where my head’s at now   TODO  ","categories": ["Engineering"],
