        "title": "Physics 2 PLTL: Teaching the Question Before the Equation",
        "excerpt":"Background and inspiration   During 2016 I became a Peer Academic Leader (PAL) at the STEM lab on Miami Dade College’s Wolfson Campus. After a semester of tutoring, I was hired to lead the Peer-Led Tutoring Lessons (PLTL) sessions for my Physics 2 professor.   It was my first experience lecturing — standing in front of a classroom and teaching for an hour. I made lesson plans, practiced every morning, and felt the nerves and the imposter syndrome like a true leader.   Process   I found that, like the lessons from the programming experience I was beginning to gain, and the lessons learned from tutoring, there were two common lessons:     there is nothing new under the sun   learning the illusion of “genius” being perspective, liberates.   I taught students how I personally learned:      Discover the wording of the question you are actually trying to ask.   Understand the perspective of the original innovator who asked it.   See the beauty.   Physics2PLTL was a site dedicated to that process. On it I built a logic tree that took students from the homework I assigned to the answers, using the same resources I had used to get there.   Take flux, torque, current, gravitational attraction. As a list of equations, they are overwhelming. But once you learn that each of those calculations came out of a context — and that out from perspective from the science that came before it — a student can sit inside the point of view of Newton or Ampère and see the real beauty of science, and of the equation distilled from it.   Result   All of my students passed Physics 2.   The site kept drawing traffic for years afterward, and I received a couple of emails from strangers thanking me for the resources.   Around 2019 I stopped keeping up with the Squarespace account, and the site was seemingly purged. Rebuilding that logic tree here is on my list.   Where my head’s at now   TODO  ","categories": ["Science"],
